   <pre> <h1>              Exercise Name: Decline Push-Up</h1></pre>
    <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>

Target Muscles

 Primary: Upper Chest (Clavicular Head of the Pectoralis Major) and Anterior Deltoids (Front Shoulders).
 Secondary: Triceps Brachii, Serratus Anterior, and Core (Abs & Lower Back) for stabilization.


</div></div>
<pre>
<h1>

Step-by-Step Execution Guide</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>:
 Place your hands on the floor roughly shoulder-width or slightly wider than shoulder-width apart.
 Step your feet back and place them securely on an elevated surface (like a bench, chair, or box).
 Fully extend your body so that you form a straight line from your head to your heels. Keep your head neutral and your core tight.


<u><b><h4>2. The Descent (Eccentric Phase)</h4></b></u>:
 Inhale and slowly lower your chest toward the floor by bending your elbows.
 Keep your elbows flared at roughly a 45-degree angle to your body (avoid flaring them straight out at 90 degrees to protect your shoulder joints).
 Continue lowering until your chest nearly touches the floor.


<u><b><h4>3. The Push (Concentric Phase)</h4></b></u>:
 Exhale and push through your palms to press your body back up to the starting position.
 Keep your core locked and your hip alignment straight throughout the movement—don't let your lower back sag.




<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Upper Chest Focus:</b> By elevating your feet, the line of push targets the upper portion of the chest muscles more heavily than flat push-ups.
 <b>◉Increased Bodyweight Resistance:</b> Shifting your feet up transfers a higher percentage of your body weight onto your upper body, making it a stronger strength-builder.
 <b>◉Shoulder & Core Strength:</b> Increases anterior deltoid engagement while requiring your abdominal and core muscles to work hard to maintain structural stability.
 <b>◉No Equipment Required:</b> Allows you to simulate an incline bench press using just your body weight and any raised surface at home or at the gym.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

 Hypertrophy & General Fitness: 3–4 sets of 8–15 repetitions.
 Strength Focus: 3–4 sets of 5–8 repetitions (if this range is challenging).
 Endurance: 3 sets of 15–20+ repetitions.
 Rest: 60–90 seconds between sets.
